The memo function ensures that the component that's passed into it only rerenders when its
props change.
So, given how simple this is, shouldn't we just wrap all our function components with
memo? No! There is a performance cost when memo determines whether a component has
changed. If the component doesn't actually do any unnecessary rendering, using memo
would result in the component being slower. So, memo should be used with care and
considered only if a component has some of the following traits:
The component returns the same output for a given set of props
The component renders often
The component outputs lots of elements
